{"name":"Kofta Brown","hex":"#883322","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kofta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various spicy meatball or meatloaf dishes of the Middle East, Caucasus, South Asia, and the Balkans."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kofta"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kofta","blocks":[{"text":"Kofta is a family of meatball and meatloaf dishes found in South Asian, Central Asian, Balkan, Middle Eastern, North African, and South Caucasian cuisines. In the simplest form, koftas consist of balls of minced meat—usually beef, chicken, lamb or mutton, camel, seldom pork, or a mixture—mixed with spices and sometimes other ingredients. The earliest known recipes are found in early Arab cookbooks, as well as in the Byzantine Empire, and call for ground lamb."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Koofteh_tabrizi.jpg/330px-Koofteh_tabrizi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kofta","wikidata":"Q752388"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Medved_mzoo.jpg/330px-Medved_mzoo.jpg?utm_sou